The LHCb detector has measured the production cross-sections of $\psi(2S)$ mesons in proton-proton collisions at a center-of-mass energy of $\sqrt{s}=13~\mathrm{TeV}$, utilizing a dataset with an integrated luminosity of $275~p\mathrm{b}^{-1}$. The cross-sections for both prompt $\psi(2S)$ mesons and those originating from $b$-hadron decays ($\psi{(2S)}\mathrm{-from-}b$) have been determined as functions of the meson's transverse momentum, $p_{\mathrm{T}}$, and rapidity, $y$, within the kinematic range $2<p_{\mathrm{T}}<20~\mathrm{GeV}/c$ and $2.0<y<4.5$. The cross-sections integrated over this region are given by:
\begin{equation*} \begin{split} \sigma(\mbox{prompt }\psi(2S),13~\mathrm{TeV})
&= {1.430 \pm 0.005(\mathrm{stat}) \pm 0.099 (\mathrm{syst})\mu\mathrm{b}},\\
\sigma(\psi(2S)\mathrm{-from-}b,13~\mathrm{TeV})&={0.426 \pm
0.002(\mathrm{stat}) \pm0.030 (\mathrm{syst})\mu\mathrm{b}}. \end{split}
\end{equation*}
Additionally, a new measurement of $\psi(2S)$ production cross-sections in $pp$ collisions at $\sqrt{s}=7~\mathrm{TeV}$ is presented, based on data collected in 2011 with an integrated luminosity of $614~{p\mathrm{b}^{-1}}$. The integrated cross-sections in the kinematic range $3.5<p_{\mathrm{T}}<14~\mathrm{GeV}/c$ and $2.0<y<4.5$ are:
\begin{equation*} \begin{split} \sigma(\mbox{prompt }\psi(2S),7~\mathrm{TeV})
&={0.471 \pm0.001 (\mathrm{stat}) \pm 0.025 (\mathrm{syst})\mu\mathrm{b}},\\
\sigma(\psi(2S)\mathrm{-from-}b,7~\mathrm{TeV}) &={0.126\pm0.001
(\mathrm{stat}) \pm0.008 (\mathrm{syst})\mu\mathrm{b}}. \end{split}
\end{equation*}
All findings demonstrate reasonable consistency with theoretical predictions.

This report details the investigation into previously unseen decays of beauty baryons into the final states $K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S} p \pi^{-}$ and $K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S}p K^{-}$. The study utilizes a dataset from $pp$ collisions with an integrated luminosity of $1.0\,$fb$^{-1}$. An observation is made of the $\Lambda^0_{b} \rightarrow \overline{\kern -0.2em K}^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S}p \pi^{-}$ decay at a significance level of $8.6\,\sigma$, with a branching fraction given by:
\begin{eqnarray*}
  {\cal{B}}(\Lambda^0_{b} \rightarrow \overline{\kern -0.2em K}^0 p \pi^{-}) &
= & \left(1.26 \pm 0.19 \pm 0.09 \pm 0.34 \pm 0.05 \right) \times 10^{-5} \,,
\end{eqnarray*}
where the errors correspond to statistical, systematic, fragmentation fraction ratio ($f_{\Lambda}/f_{d}$), and normalization channel ($B^0 \rightarrow K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S}\pi^{+}\pi^{-}$) uncertainties, respectively. Additionally, a preliminary measurement of the $CP$ asymmetry yields:
\begin{eqnarray*}
  A_{C/!P} (\Lambda^0_{b} \rightarrow \overline{\kern -0.2em K}^0 p \pi^{-}) &
= & 0.22 \pm 0.13\mathrm{\,(stat)} \pm 0.03\mathrm{\,(syst)} \, .
\end{eqnarray*}
No significant signals were detected for $\Lambda^0_{b} \rightarrow K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S}p K^{-}$ decays, $\Xi^{0}_{b}$ decays to either $K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S}p \pi^{-}$ or $K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S}p K^{-}$ final states, or the $\Lambda^0_{b} \rightarrow D^{-}_{s} (\rightarrow K^0_{\rm\scriptscriptstyle S} K^{-}) p$ decay; consequently, upper limits on their branching fractions are provided.

This paper presents an extension of rectangular metric spaces, termed controlled rectangular metric spaces, by modifying the rectangular inequality to: \begin{equation*}
  d(x, y)\leq\alpha(x, u)d(x, u)+\alpha(u, v)d(u, v)+\alpha(v, y)d(v, y),
\end{equation*} which holds for all distinct $x, y, u, v\in X$ where $\alpha: X\times X\rightarrow[1,\infty[$. Furthermore, we derive several fixed point theorems for self-mappings within these spaces. Our primary findings generalize and enhance numerous existing results in the field. Additionally, an illustrative example is provided to validate the obtained theorems.

This study investigates the multiplicity of normalized solutions for the Schrödinger equation featuring mixed nonlinearities, defined by the system:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases} -\Delta u=\lambda u+h(\epsilon x)|u|^{q-2}u+\eta |u|^{p-2}u,\quad
x\in \mathbb{R}^N, \\ \int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|u|^2dx=a^2, \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where $a, \epsilon, \eta>0$, $q$ falls below the $L^2$-critical threshold, $p$ exceeds it, $\lambda$ serves as an unknown Lagrange multiplier in $\mathbb{R}$, and $h$ is a positive, continuous function. The analysis demonstrates that for sufficiently small $\epsilon$, the count of normalized solutions is bounded below by the number of global maxima of $h$. Additionally, the orbital stability of these derived solutions is examined. Notably, the findings encompass the Sobolev critical scenario where $p=2N/(N-2)$.

We examine the following first-order differential system:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{l}
\dot{x}= y, \qquad \dot{y}= -x+a(1-y^{2n})y,
\end{array}
\end{equation*}
where $a$ is a real parameter and the dots represent derivatives with respect to time $t$. This system, referred to as the generalized Rayleigh system, arises, for example, in modeling diabetic chemical processes within a constant area duct, accounting for heat addition or rejection. In this study, we characterize the global dynamics of this generalized Rayleigh system. Specifically, we establish that a unique limit cycle exists when the parameter $a\ne 0$.

