u,0}$. Finally, we outline future prospects offered by complementary probes, such as galaxy surveys, dedicated neutrino mass experiments, and facilities aimed at directly detecting the cosmic neutrino background.

The Koebe problem for univalent polynomials with real coefficients has been completely resolved only for trinomials, where both the Koebe radius and the extremal polynomial have been identified. While the general case remains unsolved, various conjectures have been proposed, including those concerning univalent polynomials with real coefficients and $T$-fold rotational symmetry. This paper confirms these conjectures specifically for trinomials of the form $z + az^{T + 1} + bz^{2T + 1}$. It establishes that the Koebe radius is $r=4\cos^2 \frac{\pi(1+T)}{2+3T}$ and identifies the unique extremizer as the trinomial:
\begin{gather*}
B^{(T)}(z)=z+\frac2{2+3T}\left(-T+(2+2T)\cos\frac{\pi T}{2+3T}\right)z^{1+T}+\\
+\frac1{2+3T}\left(2+T-2T\cos\frac{\pi T}{2+3T}\right)z^{1+2T}. \end{gather*}
Key words and phrases: Koebe one-quarter theorem, Koebe radius, univalent polynomial, trinomials with fold symmetry.
